  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Pools of liquid mercury sat under a Mexican pyramid. Teotihuacan, north-east of Mexico City. Seventeen metres down, sealed for about eighteen centuries.",
        "visual_query": "mexican pyramid stone steps",
        "visual_fallback": "stone pyramid"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Heavy rain opened a sinkhole in 2003 beside the Temple of the Feathered Serpent. Below it, a shaft, then a corridor almost a hundred metres long.",
        "visual_query": "dark narrow tunnel excavation",
        "visual_fallback": "tunnel passage"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The walls were coated in pyrite and magnetite so the passage glittered. Someone built a miniature landscape down there, with mercury for lakes. Why bury a map of the underworld?",
        "visual_query": "glittering mineral pyrite rock",
        "visual_fallback": "shiny mineral"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Nobody knows who these people were, what language they spoke, or what they called this city. The Aztecs named it centuries after it fell.",
        "visual_query": "teotihuacan avenue ruins mexico",
        "visual_fallback": "ancient avenue"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Mercury is vanishingly rare in Mesoamerica. Sergio Gomez thinks it stood for an underworld river, and that a royal tomb may be close.",
        "visual_query": "archaeologist working underground site",
        "visual_fallback": "archaeology dig"
      }
    ],
